---
import { ArrowRight } from '@lucide/astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/routes';
---

<section class="intro-overview" aria-labelledby="intro-overview-heading">
    <div class="intro-overview__heading">
        <div>
            <p class="section-kicker">How the game organizes status</p>
            <h2 id="intro-overview-heading">Ranks and Titles are two different appointment tracks.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
            <em>Rise to Power</em> lets an officer hold a Rank and a Title at the same
            time. The two systems come from different political traditions, represent
            different kinds of recognition, and improve different character stats.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div
        class="intro-overview__logic"
        aria-label="How Ranks and Titles work in Rise to Power"
    >
        <article>
            <span class="intro-overview__seal" aria-hidden="true">官</span>
            <p class="section-kicker">Imperial Court track</p>
            <h3>Rank <small>(game term)</small></h3>
            <p>
                The game groups court-derived appointments under <strong>Rank</strong>:
                great ministers, ministries and bureaus, palace guards, provincial
                offices, and other positions rooted in the Imperial Court system.
            </p>
            <strong>Raises Politics ↑</strong>
        </article>

        <span class="intro-overview__plus" aria-hidden="true">+</span>

        <article>
            <span class="intro-overview__seal" aria-hidden="true">将</span>
            <p class="section-kicker">Warrior-government track</p>
            <h3>Title</h3>
            <p>
                Titles come from the warrior-government side: offices and appointments
                such as Shōgun, Kanrei, Tandai, and Shugo associated with the
                Muromachi shogunate and its regional administration.
            </p>
            <strong>Raises Leadership ↑</strong>
        </article>
    </div>

    <p class="intro-overview__coexistence">
        These are parallel systems, not upgrades from one into the other. An officer may
        hold both at once.
    </p>

    <div class="intro-overview__clarification">
        <div class="intro-overview__clarification-copy">
            <p class="section-kicker">The important historical correction</p>
            <h3>“Rank” is a game label, not the literal historical category.</h3>
            <p>
                Historically, <strong lang="ja">位階</strong> (<em>ikai</em>, court rank)
                and <strong lang="ja">官職</strong> (<em>kanshoku</em>, court office)
                were distinct. A court rank was a person’s formal grade in the hierarchy;
                a court office was the post that person held.
            </p>
            <p>
                The classical system associated offices with expected ranks through
                <strong lang="ja">官位相当</strong>, but the two were not identical.
                <em>Rise to Power</em> simplifies that history by placing court offices
                and related court status together in one gameplay category called
                <strong>Ranks</strong>.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="intro-overview__terms" aria-label="Court rank and court office distinction">
            <div>
                <span lang="ja">位階</span>
                <strong>Court rank</strong>
                <small>A person’s formal grade and precedence.</small>
            </div>
            <div>
                <span lang="ja">官職</span>
                <strong>Court office</strong>
                <small>A specific post, duty, or title within government.</small>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="intro-overview__example">
        <div>
            <p class="section-kicker">One province, two systems</p>
            <h3>Mutsu makes the distinction visible.</h3>
            <p>
                Similar English translations can make the two traditions look alike.
                The Japanese names show that these are different appointments issued
                through different political systems.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="intro-overview__comparison">
            <div>
                <strong>Governor of Mutsu</strong>
                <span lang="ja">陸奥守</span>
                <small>Imperial Court office → Rank → Politics</small>
            </div>
            <div>
                <strong>Shugo of Mutsu</strong>
                <span lang="ja">陸奥守護</span>
                <small>Shogunate appointment → Title → Leadership</small>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="intro-overview__title-note">
        <p class="intro-overview__jp" lang="ja">信長の野望・天下創世</p>
        <div>
            <p class="section-kicker">Name note</p>
            <h3><em>Tenka Sōsei</em> and <em>Rise to Power</em></h3>
            <p>
                <em>Rise to Power</em> is the English release title. <em>Tenka Sōsei</em>
                is the Japanese subtitle; <em>tenka</em> evokes the realm or “all under
                heaven,” while <em>sōsei</em> means creation or genesis.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <a class="intro-overview__more" href={sitePath('trivia/context/')}>
        <span>Read the full political context</span>
        <ArrowRight size={15} stroke-width={1.9} aria-hidden="true" />
    </a>
</section>
